# Aggregation metadata history

The first draft came close to identifying `lib/Elastica/QueryBuilder/DSL/Aggregation.php` as the implementation location for the aggregation metadata lifecycle. After review, the old wording remains as a record of the search approach used at the time, but no longer serves as a conclusion about responsibility: the issue name and call name lead people to this file, which does not mean every step involving metadata happens here.

The file itself remains a useful entry point. The repository index lists it as an existing PHP module that can be used for reading the code and checking historical behavior. The direct dependencies visible at the top of the file are:

```text
use Elastica\Aggregation\Avg;
use Elastica\Aggregation\AvgBucket;
use Elastica\Aggregation\BucketScript;
use Elastica\Aggregation\Cardinality;
```

The first approach is to infer from the path that the DSL file is responsible for the entire lifecycle. This is convenient, but also makes it easy to stitch class names, file locations, and runtime actions into a story without evidence. The second approach is to confirm only the local declarations, keep the path as a starting point for follow-up checks, and continue looking for actual calls before deciding at which step metadata appears and which party uses it. The decision here is to stick with the second approach.

This choice does not mean the file is unrelated. The aggregation class imports explain why the earlier investigation stopped here; they also give readers enough to start investigating from the aggregation DSL. But the existing record does not identify the caller, the serialization point, or where the results are consumed, so those objects cannot be filled in from these four class names.

The follow-up review was interrupted by an issue with an old-version image. ESC-216 moved to Blocked because the required old-version image had been removed, then returned to To Do. This process produced no new call evidence; it only showed that the original quick assessment could not be expanded on further. A correction was also added to the public record: in the Aggregation DSL section, one point in the aggregation metadata lifecycle was still unconfirmed.

This version therefore keeps two levels distinct: the path is an entry point for verification; the full lifecycle remains an open question. The most recent actual call, how it was made, and the result the user saw still need to be added.
